IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org
 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Rankine-4-%C2%A9-Dirk-Skiba-scaled-for-website
 .jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Rankine-4-%C2%A9-Dirk-Skiba-scaled-for-website.jpg
UID:skaQZO-9wJwmqRScXlF55
SUMMARY:Inprint Claudia Rankine Reading
DTSTAMP:20260730T054953Z
DTSTART:20260914T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:General admission tickets will go on sale on Tuesday\, August 2
	5\, 2026 at 12 pm. Season tickets are on sale here. \nClaudia Rankine will
	 read from her new hybrid-genre work Triage\, followed by an on-stage conv
	ersation led by poet and Rice University professor Phillip B. Williams. Th
	e evening will conclude with a book sale and signing. The event is present
	ed as part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett Root Brown Reading Series.  
	\nWhether writing about intimacy or alienation\, Claudia Rankine possesses
	 a voice of unrelenting candor that “shimmers with wisdom and fury” (The T
	elegraph). A MacArthur Fellow\, Rankine is the author of five collections 
	of poetry\, including her bestselling Citizen: An American Lyric\, which e
	xplores what it means to be an American citizen in a “post-racial” society
	. A defining text for our time\, Citizen won the National Book Critics Cir
	cle Award\, the NAACP Image Award\, the PEN Open Book Award\, and the Los 
	Angeles Times Book Prize. She is also the author of three plays\, includin
	g Provenance of Beauty: A South Bronx Travelogue\, which is performed on a
	 bus ride through the Bronx\; and is the editor of several anthologies\, i
	ncluding The Racial Imaginary: Writers on Race in the Life of the Mind. \n
	Rankine returns to Inprint with her genre-defying new work Triage\, which 
	collides the lyric with narrative\, memoir\, image\, and essay to offer he
	r most personal and emotionally resonant writing yet. Triage follows the t
	urbulent friendship between two characters\, the narrator and the theorist
	\, self-identified sisters struggling to define their wounded histories an
	d their shared but separate lives. According to Jamaica Kincaid\, “Claudia
	 Rankine’s remarkable\, outstanding odyssey—an account of our present\, wh
	ich is also our past\, which is always our future—is beautifully rendered 
	in a kaleidoscope of images and sensations. . . . Triage is exquisite.” \n
	\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-claudia-rankine-reading-2/\n\
	nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-claudia-rankine-reading-2/
LOCATION:Brockman Hall for Opera\, Rice University\, Entrance #18 off Rice 
	Boulevard\, Houston\, TX\, 77005
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
